\subsection{Single-Column Model Verification and Observation Comparison}
\label{subsec:numerical_verification}

We verify the prognostic $4\text{D}$ GSPT SBL formulation by comparing Single-Column Model (SCM) simulations against high-resolution observational data from three contrasting field campaigns: the Cooperative Atmosphere-Surface Exchange Study 1999 (\textbf{CASES-99}), the Fluxes Over Snow Surfaces (\textbf{FLOSS}) experiment, and the Surface Heat Budget of the Arctic Ocean (\textbf{SHEBA}) campaign.

\paragraph{Physical Setup and Surface Boundary Drag.}
The physical differences between these environments lie in their surface boundary structures, aerodynamic roughness, and thermal insulation profiles. Simulations are initialized under clear nocturnal radiative cooling conditions, varying the aerodynamic roughness length $z_{0m}$ and skin layer thermal capacity $C_{\mathrm{skin}}$:
Thermal-response sensitivity is parameterized consistently by $\beta_T$ through $G(T_s)$, while the fast linear TKE shift uses $\sigma_e$, matching the canonical governing notation.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{CASES99 (Grassland Plains):} Featuring open terrain with moderate roughness ($z_{0m} \approx 0.02\,\mathrm{m}$), the surface exerts a continuous frictional torque on the wind field. This open-terrain regime allows trajectories to execute wide, unconstrained global dynamic loops in $(U,V)$ phase space, driven by Blackadar inertial acceleration following frictional decoupling.
    \item \textbf{FLOSS (Fluxes Over Snow Surfaces):} Smooth snow cover features an exceptionally low roughness length ($z_{0m} \approx 1.0 \times 10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m}$) combined with intense thermal insulation from the underlying snowpack. The snowpack suppresses mechanical shear and isolates the surface from soil heat flux, acting as a strong dynamic damper that rapidly quenches TKE and pins the state vector tightly to the laminar residual sheet $\mathcal{S}_0^{\mathrm{lam}}$.
    \item \textbf{SHEBA (Arctic Pack Ice):} The perennial sea ice pack presents an extreme polar environment ($z_{0m} \approx 1.0 \times 10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m}$) with strong seasonal variation in net radiation. To capture deep vertical shear profiles over the ice sheet, the master length scale is expanded ($l_0 = 15.0\,\mathrm{m}$) with shear efficiency $\eta = 1.5$, stabilizing the boundary layer against immediate collapse during prolonged polar nights.
\end{itemize}

