\paragraph{Geometric Resolution of the RMSE Paradox.}
The empirical regression metrics in Table~\ref{tab:site_comparison_metrics} reveal a counter-intuitive finding termed the \textbf{Geometric RMSE Paradox}: FLOSS exhibits a lower manifold fit error ($T_s$ RMSE) than CASES-99, despite CASES-99 capturing a higher percentage of physical state-space trajectories ($94.2\%$).

This paradox is resolved geometrically in Figure~\ref{fig:phase_space_rmse_paradox}. In FLOSS, the snowpack interface acts as a strong dynamic damper, pinning the state vector tightly to the laminar manifold sheet $\mathcal{S}_0^{\mathrm{lam}}$ and yielding highly predictable, radiation-dominated surface states with low error variance. Conversely, in CASES-99, low surface damping allows the system to explore wide, unconstrained global dynamics—tracing large-amplitude Blackadar inertial loops across non-linear state space. Standard point-wise RMSE metrics penalize these unconstrained physical excursions, mistaking non-linear trajectory exploration for model error.

