\paragraph{Tri-Height Convergence as an Emergent Collapse Marker.}
Figure~\ref{fig:triheight_hovmoller_convergence} presents time-height Hovmöller diagnostics linking manifold transitions directly to vertical column structure. The three independent height trackers—diffusivity cutoff ($h_D$), TKE floor ($h_e$), and TKE gradient maximum ($h_{\partial e}$)—converge to a single altitude during collapse intervals ($h_D \approx h_e \approx h_{\partial e}$). This \textbf{triple-point convergence} confirms that tri-height diagnostics mark an emergent geometric interface (capping inversion boundary) rather than uncoupled heuristic fits.

